## Decision framework summary

| Question | Recommended posture |
|---|---|
| Should the company take sides? | Only where direct involvement, established values, legal duties, stakeholder harm, and ability to act justify a position |
| Should every region issue its own position? | No; regions may localize expression but not facts or core commitments |
| Should the company remain neutral? | Avoid unnecessary political adjudication, but do not use neutrality to evade responsibility for company conduct or human harm |
| How should misinformation be handled? | Correct material falsehoods quickly with concise, verifiable facts |
| How should franchisee demands be managed? | Provide participation, protection, and local flexibility within binding brand boundaries |
| How should employee pressure be handled? | Listen, explain the framework, protect respectful expression, and prevent harassment or compelled political endorsement |
| When should the brand speak globally? | When the company is materially implicated, stakeholders face serious harm, or regional inconsistency threatens credibility |
| When should it act operationally? | When safety, law, human rights, or the integrity of its commitments requires more than communication |
| What should remain consistent? | Facts, legal compliance, human dignity, non-discrimination, safety, and corporate accountability |
| What may vary locally? | Language, spokesperson, examples, timing, channels, and emphasis |
| How should success be judged? | By trust, safety, resilience, factual understanding, and recovery—not by eliminating all negative sentiment |

## Conclusion

A global brand cannot resolve geopolitical conflict through communications, and it should not promise universal political alignment. It can, however, control whether its own behavior is coherent, factual, lawful, humane, and operationally credible.

The strongest model is neither rigid global uniformity nor unrestricted local autonomy. It is **one enterprise standard with disciplined local expression**:

- One factual record;
- One set of core principles;
- Clear thresholds for speaking;
- Central authority for high-risk positions;
- Local input and culturally credible delivery;
- Protection for employees and franchisees;
- Rapid correction of misinformation;
- Operational action where the company is responsible or capable of helping;
- Transparent acknowledgment of unavoidable trade-offs.

Some consumers may continue to boycott regardless of the response. The company should not chase perfect approval through contradictory promises. It should aim to be able to explain, in every market, **what it did, why it did it, which principle governed the decision, and why that principle would still apply if the political circumstances were reversed**.
